Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is the 'ability to consummate' concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: 'Material Adverse Effect' means any change, event, effect or circumstance (each, an 'Effect') that, individually or in combination, (a) has had or would reasonably be expected to have a material adverse effect on the business, assets, liabilities, financial condition or results of operations of the Company and its Subsidiaries, considered as a whole, or (b) would, or would reasonably be expected to, prevent, materially delay or materially impair the ability of the Company to consummate the transactions contemplated by this Agreement; provided, however, that, in the case of clause (a) only, none of the following shall be deemed to be or constitute a Material Adverse Effect or be taken into account: (i) changes in general economic conditions; (ii) changes in conditions in the financial markets; (iii) changes in conditions in the industries in which the Company operates; (iv) acts of war, terrorism, or natural disasters; (v) changes in laws or regulations; (vi) any failure by the Company to meet projections; and (vii) any action taken with the written consent of Parent. The exclusions listed do not apply to clause (b), which is specifically excluded from such carveouts. (Pages 10-11)\nAnswer:
